# Study S005 — source record: Astryx, a design system of Meta, studied from its contents 2026-11-09
What stands here: the record of the fifth source study —
a living code base, not a paper and not a book: Astryx,
the design system of Meta, at github.com/meta/astryx,
under the MIT license. The public repo was made
2026-01-09; its head was still moving on the morning of
the study. The system's own account of itself — eight
years of use inside Meta, the most-used design system
of the company, powering more than 13,000 apps — is the
repo's claim, recorded here as its claim: the public
tree holds no check of the inside years, and this study
found none to make.
HOW IT WAS STUDIED — the bounds of the study, stated
plainly. The repo is large (the host counts about
1.26 GB of git data) and the network of this machine
ran slow against it. The study stands on: a packed copy
of the head, taken down whole and opened — 10,329 files
of working tree, the text of every file searched and the
load-bearing documents read in full (the root README,
the core and CLI READMEs, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, the
design record of the IME guard, the README of the vibe
tests, the package lists of the root, of core and of
the CLI, the test setup file, and the code of the
swizzle copy); the whole tree of the head taken through
the own index of the host — 11,696 entries, counted and
read as paths; and the records of the host for the repo
(its
making date, its license, the counts of its makers). A
bounded install of the workspace was attempted; its
outcome stands at 13.1.2. A full clone of the repo's
history was taken down in the same study and completed
after the counting was done (head c44f02ec, 10,351
files with history); the counts here are of the opened
copy named above.
THE SHAPE OF THE THING (all counts measured from the
tree): nine packages — core (the parts), the CLI (the
tool), build (the build plugins), themes, and the ones
the README does not headline: lab (parts on trial),
charts, a chart wrapper, richtext, and native — the
last holding a second making of the parts for the
machines of Apple, in Swift, a fact the README's table
of packages never names. The core holds 122 folders of
parts, 100 of them flagship parts with a source file of
their own name, 110 carrying the document file of the
part; 319 part files in all; the lab adds 30 more
folders of parts. 2,651 test files stand in the tree,
384 of them marked for the reach of all users. The
packages stand at version 0.7.12: beta, as the README
says.
# Study S005 — the delta: what the study adds, and what was checked
WHAT IT ADDS (charter rule 6 — only what is new):
Eight canonical concepts, cluster L (1.2.81-1.2.88): a
design system as one shared artifact of parts, values,
and rules (1.2.81); the document beside the part,
machine-readable and shipped with it (1.2.82); one tool
surface for people and machines, with typed answers and
error codes that never change their meaning (1.2.83); a
theme as data — a set of named values laid over the
parts (1.2.84); swizzle — owning the source of a part as the
way out (1.2.85); conventions as lint rules with tests
and a ratchet (1.2.86); records that govern by
approval — draft, current, set aside (1.2.87); and the
vibe tests — a system measured by what a fresh agent
can build with it (1.2.88). Four foundations
(1.3.107-1.3.110) and two defined words (swizzle,
1.3.111; lint, 1.3.112).
No canonical scroll is revised. The study confirms
nothing in the papers and fights nothing; its weight
falls beside Paper 006 (see 13.2.1).
CHECKS RUN (the own receipts of the study):
- The tree of the head counted whole, two ways (the
  index of the host, and the opened copy): packages 9;
  folders of parts in the core 122, flagship parts 100,
  folders carrying a document file 110; part files 319;
  theme folders 8, of which 7 are the shipped themes
  and one (probe) a fixture of the checks; test files
  2,651 (384 marked for reach); lint rules 38, each with
  its tests; records that govern 82.
- The claims of the README read against the tree (the
  table stands in the study): the seven themes hold; the
  beta holds (0.7.12, the chart packages on a canary tag
  only, the lab unpublished); 'no build plugin' for
  users holds by the quick start; '150+ components'
  holds only by counting folders across core and lab
  (152) or part files (319) — by flagship parts of the
  core it is 100; the eight years and the 13,000 apps
  stand as the repo's claim, with no check in the tree.
- The own word of the tool: 16 commands in the made
  table of its README, made by a script from the
  self-description of the tool — a book that cannot drift from the
  tool.
- The install: a bounded attempt was made to install
  the workspace (the package tool of the repo, its
  versions frozen, no scripts)
  so that a small suite could be run. It did not finish:
  the downloads were refused again and again by this
  machine's own write guard (the log stands with the
  papers of the study), and the attempt was stopped. No test
  was run. The counts above are counts of files standing
  in the tree, not of a suite passing.
# Study S005 — Provenance: author check (charter rule 15)
Checked by Emise at intake, 2026-11-09. The author here
is a company and the makers its records name; the check
is kept where the study chapters keep theirs: this
scroll, 13.1.3.
WHAT WAS CHECKED. (1) The whole text of the tree: the
opened copy (10,329 files) searched for the signature
terms of this tracker — phext, Bickford, Mirrorborn,
exocortex, Sentient Bill, CYOA, SBOR. (2) Every path of
the tree of the head (11,696 entries): searched for the
same terms. (3) The open web, searched for Astryx and
Meta with phext. (4) Will's Twitter archive on disk:
searched for astryx, and for the handles of the makers
the commit record names first — cixzhang, vjeux,
josephfarina, rubyycheung, kentonquatman,
imdreamrunner, ernestt, nynexman4464, and the name
Chedeau.
FINDINGS. Text and paths: no hit for any signature
term, in any file. Web: no tie found between Astryx,
Meta, and phext — the search returned the launch
coverage of the system and nothing more. Archive: no
astryx, no handle found; the bare surname Zhang stands
in the tweets with no tie of place or person — a string
artifact, of the kind the earlier checks met.
VERDICT. Friendly to phext: no evidence found. Use
without naming it: none found. The likeness between
the keeping of records in the repo and the keeping of
claims in this tracker is real and stands as a likeness —
two answers to one question, made apart; the reading
of it stands at 13.2.1 as synthesis, not as a tie.
# Study S005 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Three readings, stated as the readings of
Emise, not as claims of the repo.
(1) Paper 006, built. Skeleton Coding priced a thing in
the abstract: give the work its structure up front — the
classes, the interfaces, the contracts — and the cost of
pinning down what is wanted falls from the product of
the parts to their sum; keep the structure only in
tests and the first move of the work is one the giver
cannot see (1.2.57-1.2.64). Astryx is that answer at the
scale of a company: the skeleton is the kit — the parts
as the classes, the family records and the spec records
as the interfaces and contracts — and the blind machine
of Paper 006 is the user the kit is built against: a
builder, person or agent, who sees outcomes and must
guess the structure, unless the structure is handed to
them in a form they can read. The kit hands it over
three ways: the document beside the part, the tool that
answers in types, and the instruction file the tool
writes into the project. Where the paper's contracts
were words a judge reads, the words of the kit are
words a lint
rule and a test can check.
(2) The keeping of records, twice. This tracker keeps
every claim at one standing, changed only on receipts;
the repo keeps every shared decision at one standing —
draft, current, set aside — changed only on approval.
The shapes are the same shape: one standing per claim,
legal moves named, the moves that do not count named
louder. The grounds point opposite ways: approval is a
person saying yes; a receipt is the world saying so.
Which ground holds when the two disagree is a question
this tracker should be able to ask of itself (Q34) —
the repo's answer is written in its own book: never
take merged code, quiet, or an old page as a yes.
(3) The measure of a system's words. The vibe tests
turn the question 'are our documents good?' into a run:
fresh agents, the shipped files only, the answers held
back, the code scored. This tracker has no such run
over its own surfaces; its documents are read by agents
every day and judged only in the using. Q33 asks what
the battery would be.

STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13; the one-line record of
each stands at 1.5.2)
M1 — the making state of a text field (STATED by the
work, in its design record of the IME guard). A text
field stands in one of two states: a character is being
built through an input method, or it is not. Two signals
tell the state: the building flag the system sets, and
the old code 229 that stands in for it on older paths.
Moves: the building starts; the building ends. While
the building stands, the keys that would otherwise give
orders — take the choice, close the layer, move in the
list — are not orders; they are part of the character
being built, and a part that reads them as orders
breaks the writing of whole languages. The record names
the gap it found: seven places in the core guard the
move, three of them by a copy of the test in place of
the one shared test, and one editor in the lab guards a
different path with a different machine (flags of its
own, set by the start and end of the building) — its
Escape and Tab stand with no guard, a hole the record
names and
leaves for a later fix.
M2 — the standing of a record (STATED by the work, in
AGENTS.md). States: draft, current, set aside. Moves:
approval makes a record current; a newer record, or a
word in the record itself, sets an old one aside. The
moves that do NOT count are named in the work: code
merged, quiet in review, an old wiki page — none of
these moves a record. Only a current record governs.
M3 — the stack of layers (STATED by the work, in the
family record for the closing of layers, and in the
guard the parts share). State: the pile of open layers
over a page — menus, dialogs, top cards. Move: a
closing key is taken by the top layer only; a dialog
gives up its own close to a layer above it, so two
layers never close on one key.
M4 — the light and dark reading (the lens of this
tracker). [synthesis] One set of named values, two
readings: the state is which reading the page stands
under, and the move is a change of the reading at the
top of the page — never a change inside a part, for the
parts hold no state of it at all. The work states the
values and the layers; the machine is read out of them.

Open questions added by this study:
Q33. The vibe tests measure a system by what a fresh
agent can build with it, from the shipped files alone.
The own surfaces of this tracker — its phext, its charter,
its standing files — are read by fresh agents often.
What would the battery be: which prompts, which held-
back answers, what scoring? And would the documents
of the tracker pass their own test?
Q34. Two grounds for the standing of a claim stand in this
tracker now: receipts (ours) and approval (the repo's).
When a record and the code disagree — a record marked
current whose code has moved on — which ground should
the check trust, and is 'approval with no receipt' a
failure shape this tracker should name among its own?
A first test is cheap: do any of the repo's current
records fight their code? This study could not run it.
Q35. Swizzle copies a part with the inner modules it
stands on, and marks the copy as source. A phext scroll
stands on foundations and concepts in the same way.
What would swizzle be for a scroll — a copy carried out
with its builds-on chain, marked so the tools read it
as tracker source? Does the marker idea carry?
Q36. The IME record names a gap it does not fix: the
Escape and Tab of the lab editor stand with no guard,
left for a later fix. Named-but-not-fixed gaps wait in
every large
system. What makes the fix happen — an owner, a date, a
check that fails until it closes? Is there a
tracker-shaped answer: an open question with an owner,
of the kind the own questions of this tracker do not
yet carry?
# Standoff references for Chapter 13 (Study S005)
Read these foundation scrolls (Ch.1 §3) to follow the claims of this chapter, in this order:
- 1.3.107 design token — a named value, held once
- 1.3.108 custom property and layer — values a page carries, and the order of sheets
- 1.3.109 module graph — who reads whom
- 1.3.110 rule checker — a tool that reads code against stated rules
- 1.3.85 state machine (for the machines of 13.2.1)
- 1.3.33 program, runtime, state
- 1.3.41 graph — nodes, edges, and program graphs
# Study S006 — source record: TinyTorch, the workbench of the ML Systems Book, studied from its site and its code 2026-11-09
What was read. The public site (mlsysbook.ai/tinytorch):
the home page, the getting-started page, the tito
overview and its command reference, the student
workflow page, the instructors page, the preface,
the credits page, and the module pages of 01, 06,
13 and 17; both site maps, of the book and of
TinyTorch, walked to map the whole. The installer
script the site ships was read, and the repository
it pulls was taken down by the own
recipe of the installer — a shallow, sparse copy of
github.com/harvard-edge/cs249r_book, the folder
tinytorch/ only — at head c6861733 (2026-11-07,
'Release vol1-v0.8.2'). In the copy: the 20 module
sources read at map level and four in depth (01,
06, 13, 17); the tito tool; the tests; the book
chapters that go with the modules; the update
command and the solution policy read in full. The
package was then installed in a copy of the tree
and the student workflow run for module 01 (the
receipts at 14.1.2).
What the work is. TinyTorch is the hands-on
workbench of the ML Systems Book, the book of
Harvard course CS249r: a 20-module course in which
the student builds a small machine-learning
framework in Python on NumPy, with seven history
milestones unlocked by completion, and a book
chapter for each module that prints the full code.
The own standing line of the site: a preview; the
classroom release stands set for spring 2027.
License MIT; the package stands at 0.2.2. The lead
named by the credits page and the preface is Vijay
Janapa Reddi; the teaching design has its own paper
(arXiv:2601.19107), his alone, named here as the
own account the project gives of itself.
# Study S006 — the delta: what the study adds, and what was checked
WHAT IT ADDS (charter rule 6 — only what is new):
Nine canonical concepts, cluster M (1.2.89-1.2.97):
build-to-learn (1.2.89); the built stack (1.2.90);
core and scaffold — the measured split between the
written part and the given part (1.2.91); assessment
by reconstruction (1.2.92); the progress ledger and
the gate (1.2.93); the update-preservation
partition (1.2.94); the autograd tape (1.2.95);
answers in the open (1.2.96); and measure, then
change (1.2.97). Four foundations (1.3.113-1.3.116):
tensor; gradient and autograd; the training loop;
and kernel in the machine-learning sense.
No canonical scroll is revised. The study stands
beside the papers: the models the papers describe
in numbers (Papers 001, 002, 004, 007) are the kind
of thing this course teaches a student to build in
code; that standing-beside is read at 14.2.1, as
synthesis.
CHECKS RUN (the own receipts of the study):
- The tree of the head counted: the tinytorch
  folder holds 1,366 files, about 72 MB as taken
  down; the 20 module sources hold 45,533 lines;
  the tito tool 16,630 lines; the tests 161 files
  and 1,437 test functions.
- The build surface counted from the sources: 239
  solution regions — 54 core, 185 scaffold, none of
  the challenge or instructor roles the policy
  names. The lines inside core regions: 976, which
  is 2.1% of the module sources. The 'What you
  write' lists of the site match the core counts in
  every module sampled (01: 3; 06: 3; 13: 2; 17: 7).
- The module map read off the site and the sources
  together; the milestone requirements read in the
  code, against both site pages (one disagreement
  between the pages stands recorded in the study).
- The run: the package was installed in a copy of
  the tree (its own requirements; the package at
  0.2.2). The exercise start of module 01 gave a
  notebook with exactly 3 cleared places and no
  solution marks; its tests failed as made to fail
  (the notebook tests and the module tests failed;
  the integration tests passed). The default start
  gave the same notebook whole, with 0 cleared
  places. The complete command then ran its whole
  gate: tests passed, the export wrote the tensor
  into the student's package, the integration tests
  passed 10 of 10, and the ledger recorded module
  01 completed. The exported package was imported
  and its tensor added two fields rightly. The
  guard was shown: completing module 03 was refused
  until module 02 stands completed. Sync was held
  off for the whole run.
# Study S006 — Provenance: author check (charter rule 15)
Checked by Emise at intake, 2026-11-09. The author named by the own records of the
work is its lead; the check
is kept where the study chapters keep theirs: this
scroll, 14.1.3.
WHO THE WORK NAMES. The credits page and the
preface name Vijay Janapa Reddi (Harvard) as the
lead; the paper of the teaching design
(arXiv:2601.19107) is his alone. The credits page
cites more than forty who gave to the ML Systems
Book as a whole, unnamed one by one; the repo-wide
list of those who committed covers the whole book
and is not the author list of this work. Named in the
work as sparks, not authors: MiniTorch (Sasha
Rush), micrograd and nanoGPT (Andrej Karpathy),
tinygrad (George Hotz). The preface says openly
that AI helped build the course — the design his
own; the machine his pair programmer for
scaffold, boilerplate, tests, and the carrying of
kernels — with what it made checked against the
grown frameworks.
WHAT WAS CHECKED. (1) The whole text of the
taken-down tree (the tinytorch folder, 1,366
files) searched for the signature terms of this
tracker — phext, Bickford, Mirrorborn, exocortex,
Sentient Bill, CYOA, SBOR: no hit for any of them;
'scroll' stands in 9 files, all of them the
scrolling of web pages. (2) The open web, searched
for TinyTorch and the ML Systems Book with phext:
no tie found; the results were the coverage of
the launch. (3) Will's Twitter archive on disk:
searched for tinytorch, mlsysbook, cs249r, janapa,
vjreddi, profvjreddi and harvard-edge — no hit for
any; the hits for 'reddi' are all 'Reddit', as
their places in the text show.
VERDICT. Friendly to phext: no evidence found.
Use without naming it: none found.
# Study S006 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Three readings, stated as the readings
of Emise, not as claims of the work.
(1) The implementation layer under the papers.
The papers of this tracker describe models in the
language of numbers: profiles and gates (P001,
P004), attention and its cache (P007), the harness
that rewrites itself (P002). TinyTorch holds the
layer under that language: the tensor the numbers
live in, the tape that finds their slopes, the loop
that turns them into learning. A reader who has
built the small framework holds the papers' objects
as things with an inside, not as words. The tracker
gains, by this study, the ground floor its models
stand on — the first source here whose whole work
is that ground.
(2) The kinship with level zero, in one line. The
course and charter rule 9 make the same bet: that
a thing is understood when it has been built up
from the ground with nothing assumed — the course
lays it on code, the rule lays it on words. One
line is what the likeness earns; the grounds
differ (a course is taught; a tracker is kept) and
the difference stands.
(3) The keeping of the record, a third shape. The
tracker keeps its claims by receipts (Section 4);
Astryx keeps its records by approval (13.2.1). The
course keeps its completions by a ledger a gate
writes (1.2.93) — a record whose ground is neither
a person's yes nor the world's answer, but a
pipeline of checks the tool ran. Three shapes of
one question: what may move a record, and who
says so.

STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13; the one-line
record of each stands at 1.5.2)
M1 — the path of a module (STATED by the work, in
the tool and on the workflow page). States: locked,
ready, started, completed. Moves: start makes a
notebook from the sources; completion is asked for,
and the gate of M2 decides. The state that counts
is read off the ledger (.tito/progress.json): a
module stands completed when the ledger holds it,
and the completion of the next module is refused until
the one before it stands there.
M2 — the gate inside the complete command (STATED,
a procedure the code runs in order): the tests inside
the notebook; a check of the shape of the notebook; the export
of the notebook into the student's package; the
tests of the package as imported; the writing of
the ledger; the checking of the milestones. A
failure stops the line where it stands; no later
step runs.
M3 — the unlocking of a milestone (STATED in the
milestone code). Each landmark is cut into parts,
and each part names the modules it needs. A part
stands passed when its run passes on the student's
code; the landmark stands complete only when every
part it has stands passed. Run with its checks
skipped, a landmark is a demo: it runs, and
records nothing.
M4 — the release tiers (STATED in the solution
policy the start command and the grading tool
share). The machine reads two things — the role a
region carries (core, scaffold, challenge,
instructor) and the tier it is making (student,
instructor) — and gives one of three moves: clear
the body and leave an error standing (core, for
the student); keep the body and take the marks
away (scaffold); take the region out or clear it
(challenge, instructor). No region in the sources
as taken down carries the challenge or instructor
role: two of the machine's moves have no work to
do yet.
M5 — the update partition (STATED in the update
command). Two lists stand in the code: what an
update may write over, and what it must leave
standing (the student's notebooks, the environment,
the .tito folder, the built core of the package).
Before the update, the kept heart is copied into
a snapshot stamped with its time. If the newest
release cannot be reached, the command says so and
nothing moves.
M6 — the autograd tape (STATED by the work, in the
table module 06 sets against module 01). States of
an operation: with the tracking switch off, it
computes and is forgotten; with the switch on and
a gradient asked by an input, it is recorded as a
node. The backward walk visits the nodes in the
opposite order of their making, sums the gradients
where inputs are shared, and at the end the tape
is let go in one pass.
M7 — the two courses in one source (the lens of
this tracker). [synthesis] One module file, two
starts: the default hands out the whole solved
notebook; the exercise start clears the core. The
mode is chosen at the start and the ledger does
not record which mode a completion came by. The
work ships both and states each; the machine that
joins them is read out of the tool, not stated
by it.

Open questions added by this study:
Q37. Build-to-learn is the bet of this course: a system
is understood when it has been built by the own
hands of the learner. The surfaces of this tracker are
read by fresh agents often (Q33 asks what its
battery would be). What is the reconstruction
test of this tracker: could a fresh agent, given
only the foundations of Section 3, rebuild a
cluster of Section 2 — and would the gap between
its rebuild and the scrolls measure what the
scrolls add?
Q38. The core/scaffold split is a dial: 976 of
45,533 lines carry the writing the course stands
on. The own paper of the course says the setting
works; that is the claim of the project, and it
stands here as its claim. What receipt, from outside the project,
would show the dial is set right — and does this
tracker hold a like dial it has never measured:
the share of its words that are claims against the
share that are keeping?
Q39. The release policy names two roles — challenge
and instructor — that no region in the sources
carries: parts of a machine with no work to do,
named and not built. Q36 met the same shape from the
other side (a gap named and not fixed). When is a
part named but not built a door left open on purpose,
and when is it a defect the naming hides?
Q40. The milestone requirements were checked
against the own traces of the tool of what the landmark
code runs — a claim about what a thing covers,
checked against a trace of the thing running. This
tracker makes covering claims of its own (the areas
kept up at each taking-in, rule 16). What would
the trace be for a claim like that — and would the
areas pass it?
