# Paper 012 — On the Instance-Specific Sharp Rates for Constrained Least Squares Estimators in the Sequence Model (Shang)

Source: arXiv:2611.11959v1. Author: Zong Shang (School
of Mathematics, Georgia Institute of Technology).
Submitted 9 Nov 2026 [math.ST]. Taken in as Paper 012
of this phext (Chapter 19) on 2026-11-10, read in full
in its HTML form: the main text, the worked cases, and
the appendix proof of the isotonic case.

THE THIRST. This is the fifth paper of the Thirst
(charter rule 17): picked by the quest from the new
mathematics listings of the day, weighed blind from
its title and abstract on 2026-11-10, and sealed in
the quest log before it was read. The author check
stands at 19.1.6.

THE DELTA IN ONE PARAGRAPH. Seven new concepts,
extending cluster O (1.2.124-1.2.130), one canonical
scroll revised (1.2.116), and eleven scrolls laid in
the foundations and the Defined Words register
(1.3.145-1.3.155: seven foundations, and the coined
words proximal, isotonic, ellipsoid and variational).
The paper prices the oldest estimator of statistics —
the projection of what was seen onto a set of allowed
signals (1.2.124) — at the signal itself, not over its
class (1.2.125). The price the field already held for
it, Chatterjee's characterization through the offset
localized Gaussian width, is true for every set and
cannot be read (1.2.126). The answer of the paper is a
method, Feature Space Decomposition: the constrained
estimator is found to carry a regularization of its
own making, at a strength lambda_self that leans on
nothing seen, and is priced through a surrogate
estimator that splits by place (1.2.127); the error of
the surrogate splits over an estimation subspace and a
free subspace, place by place, by one inequality
(1.2.128). Read for three
geometries the price is explicit, and for the monotone
cone it is a sum over adaptive local windows, priced
by local variation alone (1.2.129); by the reduction of Han
the isotonic price crosses to regression at an
effective noise (1.2.130).
# The paper's formal core, as stated by the paper

The setting. The sequence model (1.3.145): a signal
beta* in a closed convex set T, seen through noise of
spread sigma from the bell law. The constrained least
squares estimator is the projection of the seen list
onto T (1.2.124). The question of the paper: name, for
the signal in hand, an explicit number r_*^2 at which
the mean square error of the estimator stands matched
(1.2.125).

The held answer. Chatterjee's Theorem 1, as the paper
takes it in: the error is matched by the maximizer
t_{beta*} of an offset localized Gaussian width
(1.2.126). The answer is general — any closed convex
set — and the paper's charge against it is one of
reading, not of truth: to use it, the width must be
priced set by set, and that pricing is hard even for
ellipsoids.

The method: Feature Space Decomposition (1.2.128), in
three steps. Step one: the first-order conditions of
the constrained problem (the KKT conditions) rewrite
the estimator as an unconstrained rule carrying a
regularization,
with a data-dependent multiplier lambda_hat — the
implicit regularization the set induces (1.2.127).
Step two: lambda_hat is traded for lambda_self, the
deterministic strength at which the mean of the gap
d_lambda stands smallest; the gap bounds the square of the
distance between the estimator and a surrogate, the
proximal step at lambda_self, which splits by place
under a small rule eta. In each geometry the paper
treats, lambda_self is the one answer of an explicit
scalar equation. Step three: the surrogate is priced
by the split of 1.2.128 — bias and variance read over
the estimation subspace and the free subspace, the
split itself decided place by place by the inequality
of that scroll. On the free subspace the signal is not
estimated: the estimator and the signal are both small
there, and the error is small without work. The
headline the paper draws: constrained estimators
approximately give themselves a regularization — the
estimator for the l_1 ball reads as the LASSO at
lambda_self in the l_1 price; the estimator for an
ellipsoid reads as a rule carrying a regularization
at (lambda_self/q) in the weighed q-price of the set.

The results, geometry by geometry. Theorem 2 (the
l_q ellipsoid, 1<q without bound): the sharp rate as
an explicit sum over places of two smallest-price
terms, holding in the regime where the rate stands at
or above a fixed multiple of sigma, the multiple set
by q. Theorem 3 (the l_1 ball of radius rho): the
same shape, with eta the soft-thresholding rule.
Theorem 4 (the k-interpolated body, the l_1 ball cut
by the l-infinity ball): the same shape, with eta the
clipped soft-thresholding rule. In all three, the
upper and lower bounds match in the regime named.
Remark 1 of the paper: the three theorems travel to
symmetric noise that satisfies a Poincare inequality
(1.3.149), the constants then standing on the constant
of the law.

The isotonic case (its section 2.3). For the monotone
cone the paper proves its Theorem 6 by an elementary
argument of its own, set out in its Appendix A: the
window sum of 1.2.129, with matching bounds for any
signal, any sigma, any N. Its Corollary 1 carries the
rate to isotonic regression in the Gaussian linear
regression setting, by the reduction of Han at the
effective noise sigma over the square root of n,
under the standing assumption the paper names
(1.2.130).

THE STATE MACHINES (rule 13). Three machines are
stated by the paper, in its setting and its method.

M1 — the projection estimator. States: a signal in
the set; a seen list, the signal plus noise; the
projection of the seen list onto the set; the
estimate, read off. Moves: observe; project; the
projection is the estimate. Guards: the set is closed
and convex, so the projection is one point (1.3.146).
Intended and actual stand together, and that is the
machine's lesson: it has no other move — no fitting
stage, no second pass — so every property of the
estimate is a property of the set. The constraint set
is the whole program.

M2 — the self-regularization selection. States: a
multiplier lambda in hand; the mean gap E d_lambda,
traced as lambda runs; the one lambda at which the
mean gap stands smallest, lambda_self; the surrogate
at lambda_self; the surrogate priced. Moves: rewrite
the estimator by its first-order conditions; trace
the mean gap; select its one smallest point; price
the surrogate that stands there. Guards: in each
geometry the paper treats, the smallest point is the
one answer of an explicit scalar equation, named for
the set in hand. Intended: a strength that leans on
nothing seen. Actual: the surrogate is priced, and
the gap bounds the square of its distance to the
projection
itself, so the price of the surrogate is the price of
the estimator, up to the matching of 1.3.147.

M3 — the FSD split. States: each place unpriced; each
place priced both ways — to estimate it, and to leave
it; each place assigned, to the estimation subspace
or to the free subspace. Moves: for each place,
compare the two candidate prices by the one
inequality of 1.2.128; assign the place by the
smaller price. Guards: the comparison is read at the
signal itself, so the split is a property of the case
in hand, not of the set alone. Intended: spend the
work of estimating where estimating pays. Actual: on
the free subspace nothing is estimated and nothing
needs to be — the machine names 'not estimating' as,
place by place, a sharp move in its own right, not a
failure to estimate.
# The paper's worked cases, as reported

All numbers in this scroll are the paper's, as
reported; the reading of them stands at 19.1.4 and in
the concepts of cluster O, extended.

The decaying spectrum. For an ellipsoid whose axes
decay as a polynomial — the j-th axis of size j^{-alpha} —
the sum of Theorem 2 reads as one power: the rate
stands as (sigma^2) raised to (alpha q + 2 - q) over
(alpha q + 2). The case is the paper's own worked
reading of its first theorem: an infinite family of
sets, priced by two numbers, the decay and the shape.

The corner at zero. For the body cut by the l_1 and
l-infinity balls, at the signal that is zero everywhere,
the paper reads two different explicit rates for two
laws of noise — the bell law, and the exponential
law. The case stands in the chapter as the mark of
Remark 1: the rate is a price of the law as well as of
the set, and a tame law (1.3.149) is not the bell law
at a different constant only — at this corner the two
laws price apart in shape.

The isotonic cases (its section 2.3). A signal of two
steps pays sigma^2 log N: the window at each place
runs until the step, and the sum of inverse lengths
reads as the log. The older reading of the field
(named there as Theorem 5) stands at zero on this
signal — its price carries a factor that any two equal
places side by side set to nothing — so the case is
the most direct showing of the paper of what the
window sum buys. A straight signal of slope v pays in three
stages: log N while the slope is small against the
noise; N sigma^{4/3} v^{2/3} in the middle; N sigma^2
at the ceiling, where every place pays its own noise
and no window helps. A signal of K flat plateaus pays
sigma^2 K log(eN/K), and the paper names those signals
as exactly the worst case of the cone: being flat is
what the cone charges most for, at this count.

The pair of one total variation. Two dose-response
signals — plateau, transition, plateau — with the same
total variation stand priced a polynomial factor
apart (the paper's Example 7): the one whose change
is spread out pays by its local windows; the one whose
change stands in a few places pays far less. The case
is carried into the concepts at 1.2.129 and into the
rider at 1.2.116: the price follows the local
variation, not the total.
# Results as stated by the paper — and the ground they stand on

The paper claims, in its own reach: (i) for three
geometries — the l_q ellipsoid, the l_1 ball, and the
body cut by both the l_1 and the l-infinity balls — the
instance-specific sharp rate of the constrained least
squares estimator is explicit: a sum over places of
two smallest-price terms, with matching upper and
lower bounds, in the regime where the rate stands at
or above the size of the noise; (ii) the way to the
rates is a reading of the estimator itself: a
constrained estimator gives itself a regularization,
at a strength lambda_self that can be named set by set, and
its price is the price of a surrogate that splits by
place; (iii) for the monotone cone the rate is
complete: the window sum of 1.2.129, matched for any
signal, any spread, any length — stated by the paper
as the first full characterization of its kind; (iv)
by the reduction of Han the isotonic rate crosses to
regression, in probability, at the effective noise.

The ground. The three geometries are priced by one
method, Feature Space Decomposition, and the steps of
the method are stated as the own steps of the paper:
the frame is the author's, from his thesis and his
joint work, and the paper builds on it openly. The isotonic theorem
stands on an elementary proof of its own, in the
paper's appendix, apart from the heavier steps of
the method. The worked cases of 19.1.3 are the paper's
readings of its own theorems, each standing to be checked against
the sum it comes from.

What the paper does not claim. It does not claim one
theorem for every convex set: the pricing is carried
out geometry by geometry, and no master theorem is
stated (Q54). It does not price the regime where the
noise stands at or above the rate (Q55). Its isotonic
rate is for signals the cone holds; signals outside
it are not priced, and the regression transfer is
asymptotic and stands under its named assumption
(Q56). And the pricing of the third step is as plain
as it is because the surrogate splits by place; a set
whose surrogate does not split is not priced here.
# Revisions, tensions and limits — Paper 012 against the standing map

REVISED — one canonical scroll. 1.2.116 (The oracle
price of judging — the floor every estimator pays)
gains a dated rider from this paper. The held claim of
that scroll: the classical theory sets a floor for any
judge, read over the class of cases the judge may
meet. Paper 012 prices the same kind of question at
one signal (1.2.125), and the price at a signal can
sit far below the floor over its class: two signals of
equal total variation are priced a polynomial factor
apart, because the price follows the local variation
around each place (1.2.129, the paper's Example 7).
The rider marks the distance between the bound and
the case in hand; the claim of the scroll stands
unchanged, read as its theory reads it — over the
class, as a bound on every judge.

TENSIONS AND LIMITS. (a) The pricing is carried out
geometry by geometry — three geometries and a cone —
and the paper states no master theorem for an
arbitrary convex body; whether the method lifts, or
the geometry is of the essence, stands open (Q54).
(b) The regime where the noise stands at or above the
rate is outside the three theorems: what prices the
estimator there is not named (Q55). (c) The isotonic
rate is proved for signals the cone holds, and its
elementary proof is the strongest ground of this
chapter;
signals outside the cone, and designs outside the
named assumption of the transfer, are unpriced (Q56).
(d) The third step of the method is plain because the
surrogate splits by place; the paper says as much by
its choice of cases, and a body whose surrogate does
not split stands outside the pricing as it stands.
(e) The isotonic bounds match in shape with constants
that stand apart — one fixed share below, ten times
above — the rate is exact as a size, not as a number.

CONNECTIONS, COUNTED. Twelve links: ten inside
cluster O, two across to held ground. Inside: the
sequence model to its price (1.2.124 to 1.2.125); the
sharp rate to the opaque price it replaces (1.2.125 to
1.2.126); the opaque price to the self-regularization
that answers it (1.2.126 to 1.2.127); self-
regularization to the decomposition that prices its
surrogate (1.2.127 to 1.2.128); the decomposition to
the window sum that reads it on the cone (1.2.128 to
1.2.129); the window sum to the transfer that carries
it to regression (1.2.129 to 1.2.130); the sharp rate
to the floor of the cluster, the class price it can
stand below (1.2.125 to 1.2.116); self-regularization
to the Onsager correction, the other trade of a
data-bent reading for one the object itself supplies
(1.2.127 to 1.2.113); self-regularization to the
corrected criterion, the other choice an object makes
by its own lights (1.2.127 to 1.2.114); the
decomposition to off-policy evaluation, the other
