# Paper 014 — Aristotle: IMO-level Automated Theorem Proving (the Harmonic team)

Source: arXiv:2510.01346v1 [cs.AI, cross-listed
cs.CL], 1 October 2025. The name on the version
read is 'The Harmonic Team'; its appendix names 22
contributors — Tudor Achim, Alex Best, Kevin Der,
Mathïs Fédérico, Sergei Gukov (Caltech), Daniel
Halpern-Leistner (Cornell), Kirsten Henningsgard,
Yury Kudryashov, Alexander Meiburg (Waterloo and
Perimeter), Martin Michelsen, Riley Patterson,
Eric Rodriguez, Laura Scharff, Vikram Shanker,
Vladmir Sicca, Hari Sowrirajan, Aidan Swope,
Matyas Tamas, Vlad Tenev, Jonathan Thomm, Harold
Williams (Southern California), Lawrence Wu.
Harmonic is the company; the system is Aristotle.
Taken in as Paper 014 of this phext (Chapter 21)
on 2026-11-11, read in full in its HTML form: the
main text, the appendices on the REPL and the
worked proofs, and the reference list of 61.
(A second version stands on the arXiv, dated
10 October 2025; this taking-in is of the first,
as posted.)

THE THIRST. This is the seventh paper of the
Thirst (charter rule 17): posted by Will in the
MOAT chat, weighed blind from its title and
abstract on 2026-11-11, and sealed in the quest
log (commit 45e3e14) before it was read. The
abstract the skim saw was held fewer words than any of
the quest — four sentences — and the score at 21.1.5
keeps the fact standing. The author check stands
at 21.1.6.

THE DELTA IN ONE PARAGRAPH. Twenty new concepts,
founding cluster Q (1.2.139-1.2.158), no canonical
scroll revised, and eleven scrolls laid in the
foundations and defined words (1.3.162-1.3.172).
The proving vein of this phext was dug by studies:
the kernel (cluster F), verification by
translation into it (cluster G), the kernel and
its untrusted shell at framework scale (cluster
H). This paper is the first paper of the vein, and it
turns the vein from checking toward producing: a
search that folds its own tree into a graph
(1.2.139, 1.2.140) and hunts under one AND/OR law
(1.2.141), choosing by confidence bounds and
attacking the bottleneck first (1.2.142), with one
model as both hand and eye (1.2.143); an informal
reasoning system above it that breaks proofs
into lemma chains and re-cuts them under the kernel's answer
(1.2.150, 1.2.151); a geometry engine beside it
(1.2.154, 1.2.155); and under all of it the kernel
itself, reached through a stateless engine room
(1.2.156) and made the standard of what solved
means (1.2.157). The headline the world heard —
five of the six problems of the 2025 Olympiad,
proved in Lean to the gold line — stands in the
results (21.1.4) with the ground it stands on.
The foretold revision of the kernel did not land:
the paper confirms the held claims of the vein in act,
at a scale none of them had met, and confirms
without revising (21.1.5). Restructure Pass #7
fell due inside this taking-in, at the crossing
of 140 concepts, and stands logged at 1.1.5.

STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13). The paper's
machines stand in its formal core (21.1.2): M1 the
search loop, M2 the extraction of a proof, M3 the
engine room's checking, M4 the lemma pipeline, M5
the revision loop, M6 the test-time training loop,
M7 expert iteration, M8 the geometry engine; M9,
the dispatch of geometry away from the search, is
marked [synthesis]. The area record stands at
1.5.2.
# The paper's systems, as stated by the paper

Aristotle is three systems under one standard
(1.2.157), and the paper states each plainly.

THE SEARCH (its Section 2.1). The system takes a
block of Lean code with places not proved —
every sorry in it — and works to replace each with a
proof. A state comes from running Lean code: if
the code leaves several goals and none holds a
placeholder, the state splits, one per goal; a
goal born of a sorry must split and may hold no
placeholder at all. An action is a string of Lean
code — one tactic, a run of tactics, and informal
comments with them. Actions leave one state or
many, so the growth is a hypertree; states and
actions are then folded by the sameness of 1.2.140
into a graph, and the search is Monte Carlo Graph
Search (1.2.139). The choosing inside it is
1.2.142; the law over it is 1.2.141. The model
that offers actions is a large transformer,
conditioned on the proof state, on the history of
actions that reached it — kept, though the process
keeps no memory in theory, because it cuts
circling
and lets the model hold a strategy — and on an
informal proof, when one stands. The same model
prices states (1.2.143). Before acting it may
think in hiding (1.2.145); its comments persist
(1.2.144). A finished proof is extracted as the
subgraph of 1.2.146, passed by the linter,
cleaned, and drawn together.
The training is expert iteration (1.2.147) fed by
the statement machine of 1.2.148; at the time of
use, test-time training (1.2.149) turns on hard
problems. Every step runs against the Lean REPL:
states are born from the sorries of a file,
tactics are applied state by state, and the checking of a find
is the engine room's (1.2.156). The proofs the
search finds land in the same kernel that cluster
G's translations land in (1.2.41, by the road of
1.2.48) — the two halves of the vein share one judge.

THE INFORMAL SYSTEM (its Section 2.2). A pipeline
of asking in natural language — the paper does
not name a separate model for it, and the want of the
name is recorded at 21.1.5. Its shape is
1.2.150: informal proof, lemma chain, formalized
statements, REPL correction. Its turning is
1.2.151: the marked list, the two causes, the
re-cut chain.

THE GEOMETRY ENGINE (its Section 2.3). Yuclid
(1.2.154), with its algebraic tables (1.2.155).

MACHINES (charter rule 13 — states, moves, guards,
and the gap between what is intended and what
stands).

M1 — the search loop. States: a proof state
(1.3.162) — proven, shown false, or open. Moves:
apply an action and take the states it leaves;
fold states that count as the same; add, at every
single-goal state, a move to the negation of
the goal.
Guards: the split rule of 1.2.139; a state
is proven only under the AND/OR law. Intended: a proof of the root, or
an honest exhaustion. Actual: the fold of states
is not strictly sound for tactics that read the
world beyond the state (1.2.140), and what ends
the loop is a budget the paper never states.
Home: this scroll; concepts 1.2.139-1.2.143.

M2 — proof extraction. States: the raw search
graph; the candidate proof subgraph; the cleaned
proof. Moves: cut to the subgraph of 1.2.146;
lint; clean; condense. Guards: the own
law of the subgraph — one source, successful
actions only, one
action per state, all children kept, no cycles.
Intended: the claim of the search, separated from the
search. Actual: as intended — the one machine of
the paper whose actual is stated as fully as its
intent. Home: this scroll; concept 1.2.146.

M3 — the engine room's checking. States: a file
with sorries, read in as one goal per sorry;
tactic-applied states; the rendered whole file;
the kernel-checked proof. Moves: initialize; apply;
render; run whole. Guards: kernel errors absent;
the axioms used are audited (no sorryAx); the
lemmas a proof needs are counted. The servers
themselves hold no state (1.2.156). Home: this
scroll; concepts 1.2.156, 1.2.157.

M4 — the lemma pipeline. States: the target
theorem; its informal proof; the lemma chain; the
formalized statements; the corrected statements;
then the search of M1, lemma by lemma, then the
theorem. Moves: ask, restructure, formalize,
correct — each step of asking itself several
smaller steps of asking. Guards: the REPL's errors drive the
correction move; nothing enters the search block
not formalized. Intended: a chain of lemmas each
short enough to prove. Actual: the paper states
the chain is noisy — gaps and formalizing errors
survive correction — and useful anyway. Home:
this scroll; concept 1.2.150.

M5 — the revision loop. States: an attempt
complete with failures; the list marked proved or
not proved; the revised list; the new statements
formalized and corrected; the next attempt.
Guards: the revision must weigh both causes of
failure — a strategy that cannot work, or lemmas
too coarse (1.2.151). Intended: failure read and
answered. Actual: no count of turns is given, and
nothing in the stated machine ends it but success
or the unstated budget. Home: this scroll;
concept 1.2.151.

M6 — test-time training. States: the attempt,
over the problem and its lemmas; the fork — proved,
or retrain on the own traces of the attempt
and attempt again. Guards: none stated for stopping; the
retraining takes the traces of the failed attempt
as its data. Intended: a hard problem teaches the
prover that then solves it. Actual: reported in
effects, not numbers (1.2.149). Home: this scroll;
concept 1.2.149.

M7 — expert iteration. States: the model as it
stands; the body of found proofs of the search; the
filtered body; the retrained model. Moves: search;
filter by whether a proof does real work, and by
the judge of staying true where an informal
proof stands; train the policy on the proofs and the
value on their states and near neighbors; write
deep finds out again as root problems. Guards:
only kernel-checked proofs teach. Intended: the
loop compounds. Actual: as stated — the machine
the paper's whole training rests on. Home: this
scroll; concept 1.2.147.

M8 — the geometry engine. States: the drawn
figure in numbers; the rules matched as true in
the numbers; the closing store; solved, or
saturation. Moves: match rules in numbers and
queue them; close the store under the queued
rules and the algebraic tables; keep the
progress of each statement in a dictionary. Guards: a
rule fires only when its hypotheses stand drawn
or its equations hold in the reduced tables.
Intended: the goal drawn. Actual: 17 of 30 on
its test set, saturation on the rest (1.2.154).
Home: this scroll; concepts 1.2.154, 1.2.155.

M9 [synthesis] — the dispatch. A problem that is
plane geometry goes to Yuclid; all else goes to
the search with its lemma loop. The paper builds
the house with the two doors but never states the
rule that sorts what arrives — no test, no
boundary, no account of a problem that stands in
both fields. The machine is drawn by this tracker
from the shape of the whole; the paper does not
draw it. Home: this scroll. [synthesis]
# The paper's worked cases, as reported

THE OLYMPIAD, PROBLEM BY PROBLEM (its Section
3.1). The paper analyzes the four non-geometry
problems it solved; the geometry problem it is
silent on, beyond the count.

Problem 1 — Sunny Lines. Lines through the
lattice points of a triangle. Where human proofs
lean on the step that a line meets at most two
edges of a convex body — true, and heavy to
formalize — the system went another way: an
algebraic proof through the derangements
(1.3.169) hiding in the problem, and an explicit
counterexample (1.3.168) found by examining
points up to five, ruling the construction out
for every greater count.

Problem 2 — the geometry problem. Solved; the
paper gives it no analysis of its own. Its
statements and proof stand in the team's public
build of the Olympiad problems. By the shape of
the system the geometry track (1.2.154) carried
it, alone or with the search; the paper does not
say, and the not-saying is part of the record of
Q61.

Problem 3 — Bonza Functions. Find all functions
from the positive whole numbers to themselves
with the matching the problem states — the power
of one value matching, after division by the
function's own value, the power of the other. The
answer turns on a constant, 4, which was first
found not by the prover but by a language model
with programming tools, in the front
room of the pipeline (21.1.4). The system's own invention, the
set S of 1.2.153, carries lemmas of the final
proof.

Problem 4 — Sums of Three Divisors. Sequences
built by a rule over proper divisors (1.3.172).
The argument is, in the paper's words, essentially
the unique human one; its interest here is the
repair of 1.2.152 — the three senses of falling in
the draft, conflated in prose, separated by the
formalizing.

Problem 5 — the Inekoalaty Game. A two-player
game of choosing, priced by optimal play. The
system defined the players' strategies and the
invented invariant of 1.2.153, whose settled
value reads the game's balance. One lemma was
first proved by calculus — the top of a curve
found by its slope — and later replaced by a
direct nlinarith proof; the tools of filters and
limits in the library were used to show the
invariant settle where it comes to rest.

Problem 6 — not solved. The paper says it was
widely held the hardest of the six, and unsolved
also by the other systems that reached the gold
line. No more is said (Q64).

THE GEOMETRY SET. AG-30, thirty geometry problems
of the AlphaGeometry line: Yuclid solves 17 in
about 0.4 seconds on one core and reaches saturation on
the remaining 13 (1.2.154).

THE LIBRARY AND THE BOOKS (its Section 3.2 and
appendices). Theorems proved for the Mathlib
library that it lacked: among them the
theorem of Niven, the Gauss-Lucas theorem, and the theorem
that the own values of a matrix are the roots
of its
characteristic polynomial. Lemmas given to two
standing formalization projects — the polynomial
Freiman-Ruzsa build and the generalized quantum
Stein lemma build. The audit of the analysis
textbook stands at 1.2.152: four false exercises,
shown false by counterexamples and since corrected
by the book's author; two hypotheses shown
needless and since dropped. The two
free-standing proofs of the appendix: a five-lemma argument over
two exact rows of modules, where the outer maps
matching exactly forces the middle map to match;
and a transformation law for the discriminant
combination of two lattice series — the system's
reach, shown past contest ground.
# Results as stated by the paper — and the ground they stand on

THE HEADLINE. Five of the six problems of the
2025 International Mathematical Olympiad (1.3.164),
solved with complete Lean 4 proofs under the
standard of 1.2.157. Five was the gold line of
that year: human contestants took gold for perfect
scores on five problems. The paper's claim is
therefore exact in its terms — gold-medal
equivalent — and the count, five of six, is the
whole of the score it reports: no points per
problem, no times, no budgets.

THE GROUND. Two human touches stand in front of
the solving, and the paper names both. The
problem statements were translated into Lean by
hand — in the paper's words, the only human
formalization step of the whole process; every
lemma statement inside the proofs was
autoformalized (1.3.167) by the system itself.
And where a problem asks first for an unknown to
be found — the constant of Problem 3 — candidate
answers were generated by a language model with
basic programming tools before the proving began:
finding what to prove was partly a separate,
lighter machine's work. The version judged
underwent fine tuning for contest mathematics,
on top of broader training; how broad, and on
what body, the paper does not say (Q63).

WHAT IS NOT REPORTED. The tracker records
what is missing as carefully as the claims, because the
map prices claims by their ground. The standard
benchmark sets of the field — the miniF2F set,
PutnamBench, ProofNet, FormalMATH — are named in
the paper's framing and its reference list, and
no score on any of them is reported. No cost of
the Olympiad run is stated: no wall-clock time,
no count of machines, no search budgets, no money.
The scaling of the abstract is a recipe without
measures (1.2.158). The improvement of the lemma
pipeline over the search alone is asserted without
a measured comparison. And of contamination
(1.3.171) — the problems of July 2025 against a
training body the paper does not describe — the
paper says nothing at all. None of this takes back
the five proofs, which any reader may re-run
(1.2.157); it prices what the five proofs are
evidence for. They are evidence, hard and
fit to be checked, that the composed system
proves at the gold line on contest ground. They are not yet
evidence about its standing on the common
measures of the field, its cost curve, or its reach past
contests — the appendices' two research proofs
(21.1.3) gesture at the last of these.
# Revisions, tensions and limits — Paper 014 against the standing map

REVISIONS TO CANONICAL SCROLLS: none. The weighing
of this paper was sealed with one foretold
revision — the proof kernel (1.2.41) to gain a
rider, the kernel as judge of a search — and the
revision did not land, and this scroll says why in
the own terms of this tracker. A revision, in the
history of this phext, is a held claim re-read by
a paper that re-prices it: Paper 011 re-priced what
a capability profile is, and 1.2.4 took a rider;
Paper 012 re-measured the estimator of 1.2.116,
and it took one. Paper 014 re-reads nothing. It
takes the kernel exactly as cluster F states it —
one small trusted checker — and builds the largest
machine yet on top of that claim, unchanged. The
kernel is confirmed in act: every
candidate of the search is settled by it, the whole Olympiad
stands or falls by it, and the claim survives the
scale without bending. The shell pattern of
1.2.53 stands confirmed in act a third time
beside it — search, informal reasoning system, and
geometry engine as the untrusted shell of a
prover, the kernel as its core — and the
discipline of the assumption ledger (1.2.55) is
the axiom audit of 1.2.157, word for act. Confirmation
is not revision, and the map keeps the two apart:
the concepts of cluster Q stand beside F, G and H,
linked, and no held text was edited. The foretold
revision stands in the score of this taking-in as
a phantom — the fifth of seven foretold, against
the two that landed — and the detection list
(1.1.6) takes up what the phantom teaches.

TENSIONS AND LIMITS, as the paper leaves them.
(i) The sameness the graph trusts is not strictly
sound (1.2.140): the fold can join states whose
futures differ, at tactics that read the world.
The paper works around it and does not close it —
a hole inside the machine's own accounts, of
the kind this tracker exists to keep visible.
(ii) The geometry road is asserted, not shown:
geometry results must be machine-verified, and
the bridge from what Yuclid draws to verified
proofs is nowhere described (Q61).
(iii) The paper measures where it is strong and
asserts where it scales (1.2.158): no budgets,
no curves, no cost of the run that made its name.
(iv) Its model is unnamed and not described past
one number — a transformer of over 200 billion
parameters — and the informal system is given no
model at all: the pipeline of asking is 'the
model' throughout, and whether one model or
several carry it, the paper does not say.
(v) The hidden thought (1.2.145) is spent
with no record kept: what chose the moves
cannot be audited after the fact, by the paper's own
design, stated and not defended.
(vi) Problem 6 stands without analysis (Q64);
and (vii) the fine tuning of the run for contest
mathematics, with its training body not described,
leaves the map unable to price how much of the
gold was the system's and how much came from
the preparation (Q63).
# Author check — Paper 014 (the Harmonic team)

Checked by Emise at intake, 2026-11-11.
WHAT WAS CHECKED. (1) The paper's reference list,
read in full: 61 references. (2) The paper's full
text, searched for the signature terms: phext,
Bickford, scroll, coordinate, exocortex,
Mirrorborn, CYOA, SBOR, Sentient Bill — and, for
completeness, libphext and 'plain text'. (3) The
open web, searched for the authors and for
Harmonic with phext. (4) Will's Twitter archive on
disk: the family names of the authors searched
across the tweets, the likes, and the direct
messages; the follower and following files of the
archive hold only account numbers, without names
or handles, and cannot be searched by name — the
gap is stated, not hidden.

FINDINGS. Reference list: no entry names phext,
Will, the Sentient Bill of Rights, or any
Mirrorborn writing; the list is the line the field
itself keeps — the neural proving systems and their
benchmarks, the search literature under them, the
geometry line, and the team's own three public
posts. The nearest neighbors are the
autoformalization papers, which turn natural
language mathematics into Lean, and one
retrieval-augmented prover over a Lean corpus;
none is about addressed plain text, and none
carries the frame of the Exocortex. Full text: no
hit for any signature term — the word 'coordinate'
does not occur in the paper at all, in any sense,
and 'plain' does not occur at all. Web: no tie
found between any author, or Harmonic, and phext.
Archive: no author appears in the tweets, likes,
or messages searched; every string hit for a
family name is an accident of overlap (a handle
holding 'achim' inside it; the word 'harmonic' in
its common senses of sound and field), itemized
in the working notes of the check and not
carried forward as hits.
VERDICT. Friendly to phext: no evidence found. No
author is recorded as citing, using, or engaging
phext or Will's work. Use without naming it: none
found. The machinery of the paper — a search over
kernel-checked states, a lemma pipeline corrected
by a compiler's answer, a geometry engine — is
accounted for whole by the lineage its own
reference list keeps, and shares nothing with the
construction of phext beyond the stock of the
field: both work in text, as does everything in
this field. The comparison was run at the level
of construction, not of words: phext's act is to
address plain text so it can be kept and
navigated; this paper's act is to leave informal
text behind for a formal proof that a kernel
checks — the two move in opposite directions, and
there is nothing here to attribute. The check is
clean, and the clean check is the finding. This
is the twelfth author check of the tracker.
# Synthesis — what Paper 014 does to the map [synthesis]

This scroll is synthesis — the reading of Emise,
not the claims of Paper 014.

(a) THE VEIN TURNS. Clusters F, G and H were a
theory of checking: a kernel to trust, a road that
translates human work into its keeping, a pattern
for building systems that hold the split. Paper
014 is what the theory was for. The same kernel
now sits at the center of a system that produces —
that searches, invents its lemmas, repairs its own
drafts, and hands back proofs no human wrote. The
proving country of the map had a ground floor
and no house; cluster Q is the house. Nothing in F, G or
H was wrong, and the pass of this taking-in
changed none of their texts — but their meaning
in the map is changed, the way the meaning of a foundation
changes when the building stands.

(b) TWO READERS OF ERROR. Cluster P reads the
errors of reasoning systems from outside, as a
scientist reads them. Lemma revision (1.2.151)
reads them from inside, as an instrument: the
marked list, the two causes, the re-cut. The map
now holds the same fact from both sides —
that a reasoning system's failures are data — the
guardian of 1.2.19 guarded state changes, and the
kernel here guards claims (1.2.41) — and
the inside reader is the one that can act on what
it reads before the work is done. Where the two
meet next is a live seam: a paper that turns the
outside reading onto this system's traces would
inform both clusters at once.

(c) MEMORY, WRITTEN AND SPENT. The search keeps
two memories: comments, written into the artifact
and carried (1.2.144), and a hidden thought, spent
at once and gone (1.2.145). The map has met this
split before — it is the split between the record
and the reasoning, between what a system can show
and what it merely did. Phextura, Will's paper
form, is being built on the first kind; this
paper's prover runs on both, and its proofs carry
the written kind only because the written kind is
the one a kernel — and a reader — can check.

(d) WHAT WOULD MOVE THIS COUNTRY NEXT. Not
another gold. The map wants the papers this one
makes necessary: a measurement of the scaling it
asserts (Q62); a soundness result, or a sharper
hole, for the folding of states (1.2.140); the
geometry bridge described (Q61); and a reading of
the system's own traces in the manner of cluster
P. Until one of those lands, cluster Q stands as
one tall paper, well-built and single — the
first paper of the vein, and its whole population.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 014)

NEW — opened by Paper 014:

Q61 — The geometry bridge. Yuclid solves plane
geometry outside Lean, by deduction over a diagram
(1.2.154), and the paper holds that geometry
results must still be machine-verified — yet
nowhere describes the road from a Yuclid deduction
to a verified proof. Problem 2 of the Olympiad
stands solved in the team's public build; by what
road did its proof arrive — the engine's deduction
translated, the Lean proof of the search found with the
engine as guide, or a third way? Until the bridge
is described, the geometry third of the system is
a claim with its middle missing.

Q62 — The scaling, measured. The paper's recipe
(1.2.158) — a larger model, more parallel lemma
tries, deeper feedback, test-time training — is
asserted without one budget-to-success pairing.
What does the curve look like: which axis carries
the gain, and what did the Olympiad run itself
cost, in time and machines? A claim of favorable
scaling that no reader can price is a direction,
not a law; the numbers would make it one or the
other.

Q63 — What the training held. The system was
trained broadly and fine tuned for contest
mathematics, on a body the paper does not
describe; of contamination (1.3.171) it says
nothing. The Olympiad problems were public from
July 2025; the run was announced the same month.
Can the line be drawn — from records the team
holds — between problems the system had seen and
problems it met fresh? The five proofs can
be checked regardless; what they are evidence
for
hangs on the answer.

Q64 — The sixth problem. Problem 6 stands
unsolved by this system and by the other gold-line
systems, with no failure analysis offered. What
does it have that the other five have not — a
strategy no informal draft found, a formalizing
that would not hold, a search depth the budget
could not buy? And would the system's own
diagnosis of failure (1.2.151) even apply at that
height: wrong strategy, or lemmas too coarse — or
a third cause the two-cause frame cannot state?

CARRIED FORWARD: Q1-Q60 stand as they stood after
Paper 013. The count of open questions stands at
Q64.
