# Paper 015 — Thermodynamics of words

Received: posted by Will in the MOAT channel, 2026-11-11.
arXiv:2611.14039 [cond-mat.stat-mech], submitted 10
November 2026. Klaus M. Frahm and Dima L. Shepelyansky,
Laboratoire de Physique Theorique, Universite de Toulouse,
CNRS, France. The Word Thermalization Hypothesis (WOTH):
the semantic interactions between the words of a literary
work drive its word frequency distribution to the thermal
equilibrium of statistical physics — the Rayleigh-Jeans
state — and the book's Lorenz, Pareto and Zipf curves are
the three readings of that one equilibrium.

THE THIRST. The blind skim read the title and abstract
only, and sealed its counts before the full text was
opened (the log of the quest, committed before the
download). The skim foretold 8 new concepts, no revision,
an axis, 11 links (7 inside / 4 across), 3 questions, and
the Oracle after at 75% (65-85), down. The reading found
12 new concepts, no revision, an axis, 15 links (11 inside
/ 4 across), 4 questions, and the Oracle after stands at
75% (65-85), down — the axis band applied as written, and
the sealed point and range exact for the third paper
running. The score stands at 22.1.5.

THE DELTA IN ONE PARAGRAPH. Cluster R is founded by the
paper — the statistical mechanics of language — at twelve
concepts: the thermalization hypothesis, the
words-particles-households analogy, the two conservation
laws and the equilibrium they price, Rayleigh-Jeans
condensation, its constraint-driven genus, the reduced
frequency, the rare-word condensate as counted in sixteen
works, the density of states as the model's free shape,
the Gini-anchored fit, the three curves as one
distribution, the Zipf window, and the wealth sibling the
machinery was inherited from. It makes no revision of
anything held: the four nearest neighbours stand as link
targets, and one of them — 1.2.70, budget follows the mass
within a context — is confirmed in act in a foreign field,
its concentration shape measured here at the scale of
whole books. The paper's own frame is stated at 22.1.2
with its machines; its synthesis at 22.2.1; its four
questions at 22.2.2.

STATE MACHINES (rule 13). The chapter carries three: M1
the thermalization flow, stated in the paper's physics —
its states are the transient, the two equilibrium branches
and the condensed state, guarded by the two conservation
laws, whose guard for texts is asserted rather than
enforced; M2 the two-parameter fitting procedure, a stated
algorithm whose later stages have no freedom left; M3
[synthesis] the text-growth machine the paper needs and
does not draw — a book is in fact written word by word,
and no state of its writing is ever drawn. All three are
drawn at 22.1.2 and carried in the machines area (1.5.2).
# The paper's machinery, as stated by the paper

THE SYSTEM. A chain of nonlinear classical oscillators:
values C_m at modes of energy E_m, evolving under their
own values and a nonlinear coupling of strength beta. The
motion keeps two quantities exact: the total energy (the
mode energies weighted by the square of each value, plus
the interaction term) and the total norm — the sum of the
squares of the values. The statistical treatment holds
both quantities fixed, and reads the equilibrium for many
modes through the two prices. The equilibrium density at a
mode is then the Rayleigh-Jeans law, rho_m = T/(E_m - mu),
with temperature T and chemical potential mu fixed by the
two conservation laws. The critical energy E_c is the
plain average of the spectrum, sum of E_m over N: below it
the temperature is positive and mu < E_0; above it the
temperature is negative — allowed by the equations and set
aside. At energies far below E_c the equilibrium
condenses: a large fraction of the norm collects at the
lowest modes (1.2.162). The paper places this inside the
wider family of constraint-driven condensation (1.2.163).

THE WORD CONSTRUCTION. For a literary work: order its N
different words by frequency, most frequent LAST in the
paper's own ordering, so the ground of the spectrum is the
rarest words. The wealth of a word is its reduced
frequency w_m = f_m - 1 (1.2.164); the household
coordinate is h = m/N, the fraction of the vocabulary
counted from the least. A token remark rides with it:
modern language models cut words into tokens (four thirds
of a token per English word on average) and price tokens,
so frequency is also cost. The remarks on the
thermodynamic limit N large are carried throughout: most
identities are exact in that limit and approximate at the
books' finite N.

THE TWO SPECTRA. RJS, the standard model, takes a constant
density of states, levels E_k = k/N, and is used for
qualitative reasoning only. RJE, the extended model, takes
the exponentially spreading ladder E_k = (e^(a k/N) -
1)/(e^a - 1), whose density of states falls with energy;
in the large-N limit its Lorenz and Pareto curves have
closed forms. The single shape number a is what the data
choose (1.2.166).

THE FIT. Two parameters, spent in a fixed order (1.2.167):
the scaled energy epsilon is solved from the data's Gini
coefficient; the bend a is then chosen to minimize the
geometric distance between the model's and the data's
Lorenz curves. Pareto and Zipf follow with no further
choices.

THE CURVES AND THEIR IDENTITIES. The Lorenz curve L(h)
accumulates wealth shares; the Gini coefficient is the
doubled area between it and the diagonal. The Pareto curve
C(w_m) = 1 - h(m) counts the words at or above a wealth;
its tail in the working window runs as C(w) about A/w with
A near 1/a. The Zipf curve — scaled frequency against
scaled rank — is the inverse of the Pareto curve
(1.2.168). The pure power law holds only inside the Zipf
window (1.2.169).

M1 — THE THERMALIZATION FLOW (stated). States: the
transient, in which the couplings still move occupation
between modes; the thermal equilibrium at positive
temperature; the condensed equilibrium, in which a finite
fraction of the norm stands at the ground; and the
negative-temperature branch above the critical energy,
named and set aside. Transitions: trade under the
couplings, with both conservation laws held at every step.
Guards: the two conservation laws — no transition may
change the total energy or the total norm. The illegal
combination the implicit statement of the flow cannot rule
out: an equilibrium claimed where the conservation laws do
not actually hold — for a text, nothing in the paper's
machinery enforces them; the quantities are fixed by the
finished book's counts, and the guard is asserted, not
enforced. Home: this scroll; concept anchor 1.2.161.

M2 — THE TWO-PARAMETER FIT (stated). States: raw counts; a
spectrum shape chosen (RJS or RJE); epsilon fixed by the
Gini match; a fixed by the least Lorenz distance; the
three curves drawn. Transitions: choose; solve; minimize;
emit. Guards: the order is fixed — epsilon before a — and
no state after the Gini match may set a parameter again
against the Pareto or Zipf curves. The illegal combination
the procedure rules out by construction: a fit that spends
freedom on the curve it then reports. Home: this scroll;
concept anchor 1.2.167.

M3 — THE GROWTH OF A TEXT (the machine the paper needs and
does not draw) [synthesis]. A book arrives word by word:
each new token changes the counts, the vocabulary, both
conservation laws at once. The paper's equilibrium is
drawn over the finished book only, so the writing process
has no states in the paper — yet if thermalization is a
process and not a portrait, the draft at every length
should already stand near its equilibrium, and approach it
as it grows. No measurement along the growth of a text is
offered. The missing machine is Q68 of this chapter. Home:
this scroll; carried at 22.2.2.
# The paper's worked cases, as reported

THE CORPUS. Sixteen data sets. Ten works in English: the
King James Bible; the complete Shakespeare; Asimov's I,
Robot; the complete novels of Jane Austen; Dickens' David
Copperfield; Melville's works; Orwell's 1984; Tolkien's
Lord of the Rings, Books 1-3; the works of Mark Twain;
Virginia Woolf's Mrs. Dalloway. Five more in their own
tongues: Cervantes (Spanish), Dante (Italian), Goethe
(German), Hugo (French), Tolstoy (Russian). And the
American National Corpus — 18,300,430 words of text,
293,867 different words, the one set written by many
hands. Word counts run from 63,958 words (Woolf) to
909,658 (Shakespeare). The frequency data were extracted
by the authors (with a language model's help, noted at
22.1.4) and are posted at the authors' own site.

THE INEQUALITY OF A BOOK. The Gini coefficient of the
works by single authors stands between 0.915 and 0.944 —
average 0.931, give or take 0.014 — with the Bible and
Cervantes at 0.944 and Shakespeare at 0.941; the Corpus
stands apart at 0.967. The paper sets the scale beside the
world: the wealth of all households in 2021 reads 0.843,
the market capitalization of the London Stock Exchange in
2020 reads 0.909. A book is more unequal than a planet's
wealth.

THE CONDENSATE IN THE COUNTS. The fraction of different
words at zero reduced frequency — the hapax share, h_min —
runs from 0.317 (Orwell) to 0.658 (Hugo), with the Bible
at 0.447 and Shakespeare at 0.378. The bottom half of each
vocabulary holds between nothing and half a percent of the
tokens of the work; the top tenth of words holds 85 to 96
percent; the top hundredth holds 52 to 76 percent (the
Corpus: 75.9). The average wealth per word across the
works runs from 4.2 to 61.3.

THE FITTED NUMBERS. The bend a runs from 5 to 11 across
the sixteen sets: the Corpus highest at 10.7, the Bible
9.05, Shakespeare 8.75, Hugo 6.55. The fitted scaled
energy epsilon is tiny — between a hundred-thousandth and
a thousandth — and the ratio -mu/epsilon stands near a
thousandth: the equilibrium the data choose sits deep in
the condensed regime of 1.2.162.
# Results as stated by the paper — and the ground they stand on

THE FITS. On the Lorenz curve the extended model's
agreement with the data is, in the paper's own reading,
nearly perfect in normal scale, across all sixteen sets.
On the Pareto and Zipf curves the agreement runs over four
orders of magnitude for the books, five for the Corpus,
including the curvature at both ends of the Zipf window
(1.2.169) that a pure power law cannot draw. Read closely
in log scale, small disagreements stand at the rarest
words — reduced frequencies under a thousandth for the
Bible and Shakespeare, under a hundredth for Hugo — where
the statistics of the rarest words are thin and the paper
says the disagreement may live there. One tail stands
apart: Tolstoy's Pareto curve disagrees at its far end,
below a ten-thousandth in the count.

THE POWER-LAW VERDICT. Fitted directly over the paper's
own window, the Zipf exponent runs from 0.999 (Goethe) to
1.17 across the sixteen works, most near 1.1, and the
fitted amplitudes stand close to the model's own 1/a. The
paper's standing rivals do worse at their own game: a pure
power law misses at both boundaries of its range; a
shifted power law spends a third parameter and, in the
paper's phrase, is not able to describe the Lorenz curve
at all.

THE THREE SUPPORTS, AS THE AUTHORS PUT THEM. First, the
steady state's stability: the sixteen distributions lie
close on one another — the argument that a book is in a
stable stationary state, robust like a thermal
equilibrium. Second, the condensate: the huge share of
words standing at frequency one matches the Rayleigh-Jeans
condensation in its numbers — the same mechanism, the
paper holds, that cleans a beam in a fiber. Third, the
reach: the same equilibrium form holds for wealth,
markets, product, and now words — systems the paper calls
completely different, sharing only the mathematics.

THE GROUND. The counts stand on frequency tables the
authors extracted and posted at their own site, the
extraction made, by the paper's note, with the help of
OpenAI's ChatGPT — the tables, not the method of their
making, are what a reader is given to check. The results
are derived in the large-N limit with the finite books
read against them; the fits spend two parameters on the
Lorenz curve and none elsewhere (1.2.167).
# Revisions, tensions and limits — Paper 015 against the standing map

REVISIONS: none. The four foretold link targets were read
against the needs of the chapter and all four stand as
links, not revisions: 1.2.70 (budget follows the mass
within a context — confirmed in act here: the
concentration it prices, the mass following the top of a
set, is measured by this paper at whole-book scale, 86 to
96 percent of tokens in the top tenth of words, in a field
that shares nothing with attention but the shape); 1.2.31
(expert polarization and cluster size); 1.2.7
(concentration phase transition); 1.2.24 (discourse-level
corpus features). The text of no held concept changes. The
seal foretold no revision and the reading agreed — the
first seal of the quest to price a revision at zero and be
right.

CONFIRMS, in the standing practice: 1.2.70 confirmed in
act in a foreign field (see above; its second
confirmation, after the arithmetic work of the fits of
this tracker). Nothing else held is confirmed or denied by
this paper; its field is new ground (cluster R).

TENSIONS AND LIMITS. (i) The interaction is never named:
no dynamics on words is written anywhere in the paper, so
thermalization is read off the shape of the equilibrium,
not derived from a mechanism — the gas argument carries
the weight (Q65). (ii) The temperature is fit, not
derived: epsilon is solved per corpus from the Gini
coefficient, and nothing in the paper predicts it from the
size of a text, vocabulary growth, or date (Q66). (iii)
The spectrum of the fit is one bent ladder: RJS is kept as
a qualitative foil and no third shape is tried, so the
bend a is the only shape the model can take — a different
density of states could compete and is not tested. (iv)
The counts ride on an extraction the reader cannot rebuild
from the paper alone (22.1.4), and the outlier Gini of the
Corpus is explained by a story about mixed styles that the
paper does not itself run (Q67). (v) The
negative-temperature branch and the model's behaviour
above the critical energy are named and set aside — the
paper's words never go there, and the tracker notes the
branch as stated-but-unused.

