# Study S007 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Four readings, stated as the readings
of Emise, not as claims of the essays.
(1) THE GROUNDING. The correspondence the
tracker held in two legs now stands in three.
Held: a claim is a type and a proof is a term
(1.2.40); the judgment — under this context,
this term has this type (1.2.42); the kernel
that checks the finished terms (1.2.41). Cook's
table sharpens each: the judgment of 1.2.42 is
the second row of the table read in type dress — a
proof of A from the context Gamma is a morphism
from Gamma to A — and the pairing the book of
Study S001 proved for logic and typing stands
extended, by the same shape, to categories. The
kernel's job is unchanged and better lit: what
it checks are the ways of the category of types,
and a way either composes or does not. The study finds:
1.2.40 SUPPORTED and MADE SHARPER; 1.2.42 MADE SHARPER;
1.2.41 SUPPORTED. One pressure, recorded as
pressure and no more: the first row of the table
pairs a proposition with an object, where
1.2.40 pairs it with a type — the study takes
the two pairings as one correspondence read in
two systems, not as a disagreement; but the tracker
now holds both ways of putting it, and a future reader
should not have to guess that they are one.
(2) THE STANDING RECORD, READ AS A CATEGORY.
The standing record (1.4.x) has the shape of
1.3.185 without knowing it. Its things are the
standing a claim can hold — true, false,
unknown, fiction — and, across the drawings of
the record, the line of one claim through them.
Its ways are the allowed steps of 1.4.6, and
every way carries its receipt: a checking
receipt for the step into true, a refutation
receipt for the steps into false, an invalidation
for the step back from false to unknown. Two
steps in a row are a way: the receipts chain,
and the chain is a proof — in the reading of
1.2.40 and 1.2.171 alike — that the later
standing may stand from the earlier. Two
honest edges to the reading. First: the chain
proves the move may be made, never that the
claim is true in the world; the receipts carry
that weight, and the record says so itself
(1.4.1) — a morphism here is a warrant for a
step, not a settlement of a claim. Second: the
reading has a hole the frame names for us —
the record writes no way for standing still.
A claim re-weighed and kept leaves no event;
the doing-nothing ways of the record live only
between drawings, in the totality of each drawing.
Whether they should be written is Q70. And one
shape the frame makes visible: fiction never
steps straight to true — the way from fiction
to true must pass through unknown — so the
most guarded border of the record is a factoring of
ways, stated in the geometry of the system
rather than as a rule laid on it. Finding,
not amendment: the charter is not changed by
this study; the reading is filed where a
future pass can take it up.
(3) THE DISPLAY POLICY, ASKED TO BE A FUNCTOR.
In the phextura form of this tracker, the
registry is to become a reading over warrant
edges under a stated display policy (the
finding L4 of the shadow's design note: the
Lean theorem that this record stands accurate
does not transfer until that policy is written
as a function, and the registry re-scoped to
claim-bearing blocks). This study adds the
next step of the ask. The policy that stands
in the converter today maps each ground to a
standing, one edge at a time. Asked as a
functor (1.3.190), the policy must also keep
composition whole: where a warrant edge backs
a claim from another, and a second edge carries
the backed claim onward, the displayed standing
of the chain must be the composition of the
displayed standing of each edge in it — a display that reads each edge
alone can show a standing that no composed way
in the warrant graph earns, a displayed true
with no path of backing under it. The identity
side of the ask is as plain: a claim with no
warrant edges displays as itself, its bare
standing untouched. In the vocabulary of this
study: the display is a semantics of the warrant
graph, and a semantics that breaks composition
reads its proofs wrong (1.2.171). Recorded as a
study finding for the phextura work; nothing in
this tracker changes by it.
(4) POLICIES, TENSES, AND THE JOINT QUESTION.
The policy essay bears on the held concepts
three ways. First, tense: a standing is a claim
about now with a history, and the record keeps
the difference between a claim that once stood
true and was stepped down, and one that never
stood true — in prose, in the events of 1.4.6.
The tenses of 1.3.188 name that difference in
logic: historically and once are the past-tense
shapes of a standing, and the prose
of the record has been speaking them without the operators.
Second, the joint question: Barth and the
others needed formal dress because a collection
of policies can fail together while each reads
sound alone. The standing record is checked
entry by entry — its Lean model prices the
ground of each standing one at a time (1.4.1) —
while the joint question, whether every claim can hold its
standing at once along the allowed steps, is exactly the path question of reading
(2). The policy work is the standing reminder
that the joint check is the one that bites.
Third, the kin concept 1.2.87 — records that
govern by approval — and the record of Section 4
— standing that moves only by receipt — are two
readings of one pattern: a record whose standing
is an operator over its own past. The study
leaves the comparison as Q71 rather than
promoting it to a claim. Verdict on the policy
material against the held concepts: 1.2.87
SUPPORTED, and the comparison opened; the
standing record PRESSURED at exactly one point —
its checks are per-entry, and the joint check
is unwritten.

STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13): none is
present in this material. The essays state a
correspondence and two families of models and
assume no machine; no implicit machine was
found. The machines this synthesis discusses
stand elsewhere in the tracker, drawn by MOAT
and not by Cook — the step machine of the
standing record (1.4.6) first among them.

Open questions added by this study:
Q69. The display policy of the phextura form,
once it is written as a function (the ask of
finding L4), is asked by this study to be a
functor over the warrant edges: composition
kept whole, the bare claim displayed as itself.
Is the functor ask the right strengthening —
and what would a display that breaks composition
show, in a case a reader could check?
Q70. The standing record writes no event for a
claim re-weighed and kept. Should a drawing of
the record write the doing-nothing ways — the
claims weighed at the pass and left standing —
so that the category of the record has its identities
on paper? Or is the totality of each drawing
identity enough?
Q71. Records that govern by approval (1.2.87)
and standing that moves by receipt (1.4.x) are
two readings of one pattern: standing as an
operator over the own past of a record. Is there one
frame — the modal reading of this study is the
candidate — in which approval and receipt are
two settings of a single operator, and what
would each system look like read in the other's
setting?
