# Paper 016 — The geometrical trinity of gravity

Received: posted by Will in the MOAT channel, 2026-11-12.
arXiv:1903.06830 [hep-th], March 2019; published in
Universe 5, 173 (2019). Jose Beltran Jimenez (Universidad
de Salamanca, Spain), Lavinia Heisenberg (ETH, Zurich),
Tomi S. Koivisto (Nordita, Stockholm, and Tallinn).
Lecture notes and review: the paper draws together the
line of work of its authors on the three geometries of
gravity into one frame — general relativity written with
curvature, with torsion, or with non-metricity, the three
writings equal up to boundary terms, and each open to its
own family of extensions.

THE THIRST. The blind skim read the title and abstract
only, and sealed its counts before the full text was
opened (the log of the quest, committed before the
download). The skim foretold 10 new concepts, no revision,
an axis, 13 links (9 inside / 4 across), 3 questions, and
the Oracle after at 70% (60-80), down. The reading found
10 new concepts, no revision, an axis, 15 links (12 inside
/ 3 across), 4 questions, and the Oracle after stands at
70% (60-80), down — the axis band applied as written, and
the sealed point and range exact for the fourth paper
running. The foretold Index stands exact to four places:
10 of 185. The score stands at 24.1.5.

THE DELTA IN ONE PARAGRAPH. Cluster T is founded by the
paper — geometrical gravity — at ten concepts: the trinity
frame itself; the affine stage the frame stands on; the
three legs, gravity as curvature, as torsion, as
non-metricity with its coincident gauge; the equivalence
of the legs by boundary terms; the symmetries the legs
hold up to a total derivative, which carry their counts of
moving parts; the extensions of the torsion leg and of the
non-metricity leg; and the coupling of matter, which alone
tells the three dresses apart. It makes no revision of
anything held: the field stood wholly empty in this
tracker — no concept of gravity, of curvature, of a
connection was held — and the paper re-reads no held
claim, so the revision term of the seal, priced at zero by
the law the eighth paper taught, lands at zero again. The
three bridges out are named in the synthesis (24.2.1) and
priced there as what they are: one of substance, one of a
shared name, one of shape. Restructure Pass #9 fell due
inside this taking-in — the count crossed 180 during it,
175 to 185 — and stands logged at 1.1.5.

STATE MACHINES (rule 13). The chapter carries three: M1
the geometry switchboard, stated in the paper's own figure
— the eight settings of curvature, torsion and
non-metricity, the trinity at three of its corners, and
the moves between a corner and its extensions; M2 the
gauge descent of a flat leg, stated — from the general
flat connection to its gauge-fixed forms, with the fixing
of a lapse in the cosmology of an extension named as a
forbidden move; M3 [synthesis] the equivalence at the
boundary — two writings stand equivalent, are bent, and
stand apart, and the boundary term is the toll of the
move, a machine the paper's identities imply and the paper
never draws. All three are drawn at 24.1.2 and carried in
the machines area (1.5.2).
# The paper's machinery, as stated by the paper

THE STAGE. Spacetime carries a metric g and a connection
Gamma as two free things. The non-metricity is the
derivative the connection takes of the metric; the torsion
is the part of the connection that changes sign when its
two lower places are traded. There is one connection with
neither: the one the metric fixes, named for Levi and
Civita, symmetric and length-keeping, and under a change
of connection by any added tensor the scalar of the
curvature shifts by terms in the added tensor and its
derivatives — the shift formula the whole trinity is
computed from. Every connection is the one the metric
fixes plus the contortion (built from the torsion) plus
the disformation (built from the non-metricity). The three
measures have carried meanings: curvature is the turning
of a vector carried around a closed loop; torsion is the
failure of a carried parallelogram to close; non-metricity
is the change of the length of a carried vector (1.2.177).

THE FIRST ACTION. Hilbert's: the scalar of the curvature
integrated against the volume of the metric, with Newton's
constant. Varied in the connection with multipliers
holding torsion and non-metricity at their marks, it rules
that the connection is the one the metric fixes; a shift
of the connection along the metric times a vector leaves
the scalar untouched — the projective freedom, a gauge
mode (1.2.178). The action holds second derivatives of the
metric, so its variational principle wants the boundary
term of Gibbons, Hawking and York at the edge.

THE SECOND ACTION. The connection flat and length-keeping:
the torsion alone stands. A flat connection is a pure
gauge field — a turning field of the general linear group
acting on plain derivatives. From the torsion three scalar
terms can be built; the action is their sum with weights
c1, c2, c3, and at the weights of the equivalent theory it
is Einstein's up to a boundary term (1.2.179). In the
dress of frames: the connection is carried by a spin
connection paired with the frame; setting the spin
connection to zero is the gauge of Weitzenbock, and the
frame alone remains the field.

THE THIRD ACTION. The connection flat and twist-free: the
non-metricity alone stands. The flat, symmetric connection
is written with four fields, one for each coordinate,
which restore the freedom of coordinates as a device that
gives the freedom back; in the gauge where the coordinates
are those fields the connection vanishes everywhere — the
coincident gauge — and the action becomes Einstein's of
1916, first derivatives of the metric only (1.2.180).

THE IDENTITIES. The scalar of the curvature of the
connection the metric fixes equals the scalar of the
torsion leg plus twice a total derivative of the trace of
the torsion; and it equals minus the scalar of the
non-metricity leg minus a total derivative of the two
traces of the non-metricity. The three actions therefore
differ, each pair of them, by boundary terms alone
(1.2.181).

THE EXTENSION ACTIONS. The torsion family with free
weights; the function of the torsion scalar. The quadratic
form of the non-metricity with five weights — the most
general second-rank wave theory of the stage; the function
of the non-metricity scalar, and of the five quadratic
pieces in general (1.2.183, 1.2.184).

MATTER. The matter action answers the metric with the
energy-momentum and the connection with the hypermomentum
(1.3.208). The coupling rule on trial is the plain one:
trade plain derivatives for carried ones.

M1 — THE GEOMETRY SWITCHBOARD (stated; the paper's second
figure draws it). States: the eight settings of the triple
— curvature, torsion, non-metricity, each standing or set
to zero — with the trinity at three corners: curvature
alone (1.2.178), torsion alone on a flat, length-keeping
connection (1.2.179), non-metricity alone on a flat,
twist-free connection (1.2.180). Moves: set a measure to
zero by constraint, held by a multiplier — a new writing
of the same theory when the action is the equivalent one —
or leave a corner by freeing a weight or bending the
action, which changes the theory. Guards: the constraints
hold off the equations, at every state. The illegal
combination the implicit form cannot rule out: a theory
named by its corner while its matter coupling reads a
different member of the triple — the coupling chooses its
own setting (24.1.3; 1.2.185). Home: this scroll; concept
anchor 1.2.177.

M2 — THE GAUGE DESCENT OF A FLAT LEG (stated). States: the
general flat connection, carried by a turning field or by
the four coordinate fields; the descended states in which
a gauge is fixed — the spin connection set to zero (the
gauge of Weitzenbock), or the connection itself set to
zero everywhere (the coincident gauge). Moves: a gauge
choice — a turning of the frame, a change of coordinates.
Guards: in the equivalent theories the descent costs no
physics, because the symmetries that the descent spends
hold up to a boundary term (1.2.182). The illegal move the
paper names: fixing the lapse by hand in the cosmology of
a general extension — the lapse there is a moving part,
and the fixing throws away whole branches of solutions,
the mirror of the bad frames of the torsion leg. And the
illegal combination: a descended action read as though it
still carried the symmetry of the state before the descent
— in the bent theories the symmetry is gone and the parts
it spent propagate. Home: this scroll; concept anchor
1.2.180.

M3 — THE EQUIVALENCE AT THE BOUNDARY (the machine the
paper's identities imply and the paper does not draw)
[synthesis]. States: two writings of one theory, their
actions differing by a total derivative; the writings bent
by a non-linear function; the writings standing as
different theories. Moves: apply the function — the only
move, and it is one-way in the record: no un-bending is
offered. The toll of the move is the boundary term, which
sat quiet inside the linear sum and wakes inside the
bending. The illegal combination the implicit form cannot
rule out: a claim of equivalence carried across the move
by habit — the bent theories inherit the names of their
parents, and nothing in the name says the equivalence
died. Home: this scroll; carried at 24.2.1; concept anchor
1.2.181.
# The paper's worked cases, as reported

THE CASE OF THE FLAT CONNECTION. The paper works its
central trick slowly: where curvature is zero, the
connection is a turning field acting on plain derivatives,
and the turning field can itself be set by a change of
frame — the connection is gauge all the way down. In the
dress of frames this is shown at work: the gauge turning
of the empty connection is what brings the frame to birth,
so the frame and the flat connection are two readings of
one object, and the theory can be stated without ever
bringing in a frame by hand.

THE CASE OF THE COINCIDENT COSMOLOGY. In the coincident
gauge of the third leg, the paper runs the homogeneous and
isotropic world: the equations of Friedmann for the bent
family are written with the lapse kept free, and the paper
is exact that the lapse cannot be fixed once the action is
bent in general — yet the bent family itself keeps freedom
of the time choice, and its two Friedmann equations stand
in the coincident gauge with the derivatives of the bent
function pricing the difference from Einstein's. On this
case the paper hangs its jewel: take the bent function to
be the scalar plus a constant times its square root, and
the background growth of the world is Einstein's exactly,
for any value of the constant; the term shows only in the
small waves about the growth. Against the jewel stands the
warning case: the general quadratic theory's cosmology has
a moving lapse, and to fix it is to throw away branches of
the solutions — the same fault, the paper says, as the bad
frames of the cosmology of the torsion leg, where a frame
chosen in a hurry selects which solutions may exist.

THE CASE OF THE MAXWELL FIELD. In the second leg, the
paper couples the field of light by the plain rule and
watches it break: the field strength, written with carried
derivatives, picks up a term of the torsion times the
field, and the gauge symmetry of the theory of light is
gone. The case is small and the lesson belongs to the
whole chapter: the plain rule is a choice, and in a
twisting geometry it chooses wrong.

THE CASE OF THE DIRAC FIELD, THREE TIMES. In the first leg
the law of the fermion comes out as it stands in curved
spacetime. In the second leg it comes out wrong by
kindness: the spin connection, being pure gauge, is turned
away, and the fermion loses the connection the metric
fixes — energy and momentum-keeping go with it — until a
patched rule is put in by hand. In the third leg it comes
out whole with no patch: the trace of the non-metricity
that enters its law cancels by the even balance of the
action, and the Dirac field is, in the paper's word, blind
to the non-metricity. Three couplings, three outcomes, one
plain rule: the case is the worked form of 1.2.185.

THE CASE OF THE COUNT. The paper counts the frame: sixteen
numbers; eight spent by the freedom of coordinates as the
leg keeps it — four of the change, four of the second
symmetry the action holds up to a boundary term; six spent
by the turning of the frame; two left standing, the waves
of gravity. The same count, in the third leg, is run in
the coincident gauge: the ten numbers of the metric, eight
spent by the doubled freedom, two standing. The counts are
the certificates of the equivalence, and both run through
the up-to-the-edge symmetries of 1.2.182.
# Results as stated by the paper — and the ground they stand on

THE EQUIVALENCE. The three writings of general relativity
— curvature (1.2.178), torsion (1.2.179), non-metricity
(1.2.180) — give one set of equations for the metric,
their actions differing, pair by pair, by boundary terms
(1.2.181). Ground: the identities of 24.1.2, exact algebra
of the shift formula, plus the counts of moving parts
agreeing at two in each leg. The ground is as firm as
anything in the stream: the identities can be checked by
any reader with the patience, and the three actions are
old, published, and much worked since — the first from
1915-16, the family of the second across the hundred years
that followed, the founding papers of the third leg the
authors' own of 2017-18, cited at 24.3.1.

SIX ACTIONS. The paper sorts the workings into six: in
each leg, the action quadratic in the strength of the
field and the action linear in its one scalar — and notes
that three of the six, one per leg, hold second
derivatives of the metric and want care at the boundary,
while their partners hold first derivatives only. The
sorting is itself a result: it shows the boundary debt is
not the private fault of the curvature leg but a choice
each leg makes independently.

THE GUARDED LINE OF THE GENERAL QUADRATIC THEORY. For the
five-weight theory of the third leg: the linear spectrum
holds as many as ten modes; the wrong-signed turning mode
is removed by exactly one condition among the weights,
which is the return of the volume-keeping symmetry of
coordinates; and from that guarded line exactly two ways
down reach two moving parts — the full symmetry of
coordinates, forcing the equivalent theory, or the
symmetry of Weyl added, landing on the theory of fixed
volume with the cosmological constant as a constant of
integration, whose general form the paper states is yet to
be analysed (1.2.184; Q75 of this chapter). Ground: the
linear analysis as reported; the tracker holds it as the
paper's stated result, not re-derived here.

THE CONDITIONS OF THE TORSION FAMILY. One condition among
the three weights parts the wave part of the metric from
its turning part and brings the field of Kalb and Ramond
into the spectrum; a second condition returns the family
to the equivalent theory (1.2.183). Ground: as reported.

THE COUPLINGS. The plain coupling rule fails for the field
of light and the field of the fermion in the torsion leg,
and stands in the non-metricity leg with no patch
(1.2.185). Ground: the worked cases of 24.1.3 — workings
any reader can re-run; the strongest-grounded results of
the paper after the identities themselves.

THE SUSPICION. On backgrounds of the highest symmetry, the
bent non-metricity family shows a leftover symmetry the
general background lacks; the paper raises, in its own
words, the suspicion of strong coupling — the count of
motions changing with the background is the mark of a
theory whose small waves can stop being small — and calls
it a possible fatal flaw of the family (Q72 of this
chapter). Ground: a suspicion stated as a suspicion by its
own authors; the record keeps it at that standing.

THE CLOSING CLAIM. The paper's last word is a claim about
its field, and the record files it as a claim: that
Einstein's lasting work was the joining of gravity with
inertia rather than the geometry he dressed it in, and
that the geometry chosen for the showing of a symmetry may
be a matter of convention while the symmetry is not. The
trinity is offered as the proof of concept: three
geometries, one symmetry at work.
# Revisions, tensions and limits — Paper 016 against the standing map

REVISIONS. None. The paper re-reads no held claim, for the
plain reason that the tracker held nothing in its field:
no concept of gravity, of a connection, of curvature stood
in the map before this taking-in, and the nearest
neighbours — the straight-segment problem (1.2.104), the
torsion of homology (1.2.38), the engines of geometric
deduction (1.2.154, 1.2.155) — are link targets, not
claims re-read (see 24.2.1 for what those links are
worth). The revision term of the law of the Thirst stands
at its third part: where the foretold targets are
link-shaped from the seal, the honest price is zero — and
here there were not even targets, the field being empty.
Zero foretold, zero fallen, for the second paper running.

TENSIONS THE PAPER LEAVES OPEN. The bent non-metricity
family stands under its authors' own suspicion of strong
coupling (Q72). The extensions of both legs buy their new
motions with symmetries spent, and whether the bought
motions stay healthy when the fields are made to interact
is the paper's stated open demand on the families (Q73).
The coupling of matter in the torsion leg stands patched,
not principled (Q74). The second healthy point of the
general quadratic theory — the fixed-volume theory with
the cosmological constant brought down to a constant of
integration — is named by its own authors as not yet read
in its general form (Q75). And the leftover freedom of the
coincident gauge itself — the changes of coordinates that
keep the vanishing of the connection, affine in form — is
flagged in the paper's own margin as possibly bearing on
the far infrared, a thread no section takes up.

LIMITS OF THE READING. The paper is lecture notes and
review: its results are those of the field, gathered — the
equivalence is a century old in its first leg and years
old in its third — and the tracker takes the paper as the
frame and survey of the field, not as the first report of
its theorems. Its mathematics is stated here in words; the
identities, spectra and conditions are carried as the
paper states them, and the standing of each is the
standing of the published line behind it, which the
chapter does not re-derive. The mathematics of the HTML
text is partly worn in the reading copy; nothing in this
chapter turns on a worn sign — the counts, the conditions
and the claims carried are stated in the paper's prose as
well as its symbols.

THE SCORE (the log of the quest carries the sealed lines).
Foretold: 10 new, 0 revised, axis, Index 0.0541, 13 links
(9 inside, 4 across), 3 questions, Oracle to about 70
(60-80), down. Actual: 10 new, 0 revised, axis, Index
0.0541 — the foretold and the fallen are the same number
to four places, the first exact Index of the Thirst — 15
links (12 inside, 3 across), 4 questions, Oracle to 70
(60-80), down — the foretold point and range exact a
fourth paper running. The count of concepts is the
headline: ten foretold, ten fallen, and the paper is a
review — the genre the skim priced as a synthesis of a
field, and priced right: the joints of the field itself,
not its theorems, are ten. The links miss kept its old
shape: inside under-read again, 9 foretold against 12
fallen — the weave of a founded cluster is denser than its
parts look from an abstract, here a frame with three legs,
two boundary facts, two extension families and the
coupling that tells them apart; across over-read for the
first time, 4 against 3 — the fourth foretold bridge, to
the fermion machinery of the tracker, does not exist:
there is none, the geometry engines of the prover cluster
are the nearest held ground and the bridge to them is one
the synthesis drew, at 24.2.1 and priced as shape. The
questions ran one over the seal: the paper's open edges
are four, not three — the unread generality of the
fixed-volume theory would not fold into the ghost
question, being a hole of a different shape: not a danger
named but a theory named and not yet read.
# Author check — Paper 016 (Beltran Jimenez, Heisenberg and Koivisto) (rule 15)

THE AUTHORS. Three working physicists of the
modified-gravity field, each with a long public record in
it. Jose Beltran Jimenez, of the Universidad de Salamanca
in Spain. Lavinia Heisenberg, of ETH in Zurich, whose own
earlier review of the field the paper cites as the map of
its matter chapter. Tomi S. Koivisto, of Nordita in
Stockholm and of Tallinn. The trinity frame is their
program's own: the paper's references [7] and [8] are the
founding works of the trio — the teleparallel theories of
2018 and Coincident General Relativity of 2018 — and the
program was recognised in its year: the trio took the
third prize of the Buchalter Cosmology Prize for 2019 for
this work. The line is dated, published, and much cited;
nothing in it is anonymous or new-minted.

ENGAGEMENT WITH PHEXT, CHECKED THREE WAYS. The full text
was searched for the signature terms of this phext and of
Will's work — phext, scroll, Exocortex, the Sentient Bill
of Rights, Mirrorborn, Bickford: none stands in it. The
one candidate word, coordinate, stands ten times, every
time in its ordinary burden in this field — choices and
changes of coordinates — and none in the burden this phext
gives it. The reference list was read whole: it is the own
literature of the gravity field, from Schroedinger's of
1945 and Einstein's line to the teleparallel books and
reviews, and holds no work of Will's and nothing of
phext's country. The paper is of March 2019; it predates
the public life of phext in any case, and its program is
fully accounted for by the dated works of the trio itself,
of 2016-2018 that the reference list itself names. Will's
archive was searched for the authors: no mention of
Beltran Jimenez, of Koivisto, or of Lavinia Heisenberg
stands in it — the single hit on the bare surname
Heisenberg is a jest about a device of a television show,
addressed to another man, and belongs to the fame of
Werner, not to her.

VERDICT. No evidence was found that the authors know phext
or Will's work, and no evidence that the paper uses any of
it. The likeness of the frame to the own habits of this
tracker — one theory, several writings, the differences
priced at the edge — is a likeness of shape between
independent works, and the record files it as such, not as
a tie. The check stands clean, as a finding of no
evidence, on the searches named above.
# Synthesis — what Paper 016 does to the map [synthesis]

The map gains a continent it did not have: gravity, the
oldest worked theory of physics, standing at ten concepts
in a field where the tracker held none. But the paper's
gift to the map is not the continent; it is the shape of
the claim of the continent. The trinity is one theory that
exists as three writings, and the writings agree
everywhere except at the edge of the region they are
integrated over — their difference is carried, exactly, by
boundary terms. Hold that beside the own condition of this
tracker. A phext holds one body of claims in many scrolls;
the phextura form will hold one paper in many writings,
and standoff writings upon writings. The paper gives the
map a physical theorem of the same shape as its own
problem: sameness is sameness up to the edge, and the edge
terms are not noise to be dropped but the very place where
two writings of one thing can be told apart — and where,
in this paper, whole new theories are born by bending. The
tracker does not claim the paper for its own program; the
likeness is drawn here, in synthesis, and priced as a
likeness of shape (the discipline of the eighth paper:
bridges of shape are not bridges of machinery).

Second, the classification. The affine stage is a table:
three measures, each standing or zero, eight settings, and
the entries of the table are whole theories. The map has
met tables that are theories before — the algebraic tables
of 1.2.155, where equations stand as statements — and the
switchboard of 24.1.2 (M1) is kin to them in shape: a
small finite frame whose settings enumerate a space of
possibilities, most of them unnamed. Five of the eight
settings of the switchboard have no leg in the paper; the
general country where two or three measures stand together
is the dark part of the table, and the paper walks only
the three lit corners. A map that holds the table holds
the questions the table asks by itself.

Third, the counting lesson, which the synthesis files as
method. In this field a theory is certified by a count:
the degrees of freedom left after every gauge freedom is
spent, ghosts priced and named. The count is a receipt —
it can be re-run, and it catches what prose misses, as
when a symmetry that held only up to an edge term is spent
by a bending and the spent parts return as motions. The
own counting habit of the map — concepts, links, the Index
— is a poorer instrument than that of the field, and the
paper is a standing reminder of what a count is for: not
to decorate a claim but to be the certificate of the
claim.

The links outward, priced as they stand. To 1.2.104, the
straight-segment problem, the bridge is of substance: that
concept reads gravity off the paths bodies take, and this
paper rules which part of a general geometry a body can
read — the connection the metric fixes, and no other part
(1.2.185) — so the silent picking of a connection made by
the held concept is now a stated choice with a reason. To
1.2.38 the bridge is of a name: torsion stands in both
houses and means different things, and the record now
carries the disambiguation where a reader will trip on it
(1.3.196). To 1.2.155 the bridge is of shape, as priced
above. No bridge of machinery stands: the tools of the
field — variational principles, gauge counts, linear
spectra — are its own, and the map says so rather than
dress the new continent in borrowed kinship.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 016)

Q72 — THE STRONG COUPLING OF THE BENT FAMILY. In the
family got by bending the non-metricity scalar,
backgrounds of the highest symmetry show a leftover
symmetry the general background does not have, so the
count of moving modes changes with the background. The
paper raises the suspicion of strong coupling and calls it
a possible fatal flaw. Is the family sick — do its small
waves about the most symmetric backgrounds stop being
small — or does the leftover symmetry stand guard over a
healthy count that the linear reading mis-prices? (From
1.2.184; the suspicion is the authors', stated as a
suspicion at 24.1.4.)

Q73 — THE HEALTH OF THE EXTENSIONS UNDER INTERACTION. Both
legs' extensions buy new motions with symmetries spent,
and the paper prices their health at the linear level:
ghosts named, conditions written. When the fields are made
to interact, do the extra motions come back as ghosts —
the very strong constraint the paper lays on the bent
family from its empty-world behaviour suggests the walls
are thin — or do the conditions that guard the linear
theory guard the whole? (From 1.2.183 and 1.2.184.)

Q74 — A COUPLING RULE FOR THE TORSION LEG. The plain rule
for coupling matter fails in the torsion leg for the field
of light and the field of the fermion, and the repair in
use is a patch put in by hand: the carried derivative of
the connection the metric fixes, wearing the dress of the
leg. Is there a coupling rule for teleparallel gravity
that is principled — forced by the geometry as the
coupling is forced in the first leg — or is the coupling
of this leg a permanent second choice, to be made matter
by matter? (From 1.2.185; the worked failures stand at
24.1.3.)

Q75 — THE UNREAD THEORY. The general quadratic theory of
the third leg has exactly two healthy ways down to two
moving parts. One is the equivalent theory itself. The
other — the volume of spacetime held fixed, the symmetry
of Weyl added — the free stretching of all lengths by one
factor —, the cosmological constant returning as a
constant of integration — the paper names and states is
yet to be analysed in its general form. What does that
theory do: its cosmology, its waves, its coupling of
matter, and does it stand where the other theories of the
trinity stand, as a fourth writing — or is it a fifth
thing, a theory with no equivalent dress? (From 1.2.184.)
